@description "Pattern: attach a hook to a pipeline (the LLM-extension shape)"

# HOOKS ARE THE EXTENSION POINT FOR AI ANALYZERS
#
# The @hook: directive attaches any named hook that was registered with
# the Kernel via action.MustRegisterHook. A hook receives
# (ctx, req, err, meta) on every invocation, on the cold path only:
#   - success → OnSuccess fires, once
#   - error   → OnError fires, once, with the actual error value
#   - panic   → OnPanic fires, then the error is recovered into an
#               xerr.AppError before propagating
#
# An LLM error analyzer is just one hook among many. The full pattern:
#
#   1. A third-party Go bundle registers a named hook in init():
#
#        func init() {
#            core.Register("ai_assist", Bundle)
#            action.MustRegisterHook("ai.error_analyzer", func() action.AnyHook {
#                return action.AnyHook{
#                    OnError: func(ctx context.Context, req any, err error, meta *action.Meta) {
#                        bg := xctx.CloneForAsync(ctx)   // detach from request
#                        go shipToLLM(bg, meta.Name, req, err)
#                    },
#                }
#            })
#        }
#
#   2. The user requires the bundle and attaches the hook once:
#
#        @require github.com/mojafirma/ai_assist
#        @hook:ai.error_analyzer
#
#   3. Or, for a multi-file project, once in a shared profile that
#      every file includes:
#
#        # shared.nflow
#        @profile prod_base :hook=ai.error_analyzer
#
#        # every other file
#        @include ./shared.nflow
#
#      The hook then fires on every action in every included file.
#      Zero duplication across the ten files. Zero changes to the
#      compiler, the runner, or the CLI.
#
#   4. The pipeline itself never changes. The hook lives entirely
#      beside it. Removing the @hook: line removes the extension —
#      there is no residue in the source.
#
# This fixture demonstrates the mechanism with the built-in
# hook.verify hook. It stamps a context marker so hook.probe can prove
# the hook actually fired on the action. A production AI bundle is
# identical in shape; only the OnError body changes.

@hook:hook.verify

@assert: result.hook_verified == true
{ probe: "input" } -> hook.probe
